---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import JournalPromptGenerator from "../components/JournalPromptGenerator.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import storyHero from "../assets/your-story-hero.jpg";
import readingNook from "../assets/your-story-reading-nook.png";
import "../styles/stories-page.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What You Make of Your Story | Dare to Break Free"
  description="Why our stories matter, what writing can help us discover about ourselves, and a collection of free writing prompts to try."
>
  <header class="stories-hero">
    <Image
      class="stories-hero__image"
      src={storyHero}
      alt="A teal journal against a lavender watercolor background"
      widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600, 2000, 2400]}
      sizes="100vw"
      format="webp"
      quality={82}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <div class="stories-container">
      <h1>What You Make of Your Story</h1>
      <p class="stories-lede">You have a story about who you are and how you came to be that person. But how much of it have you actually stopped to question?</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="stories-section stories-section--explore" aria-labelledby="stories-know-heading">
    <div class="stories-container stories-split stories-knowing">
      <div class="stories-prose">
        <h2 id="stories-know-heading">Getting to know ourselves</h2>
        <p>I've often wondered how much of our struggles come from being disconnected from ourselves. We're so busy getting on with life, responding to what's happening around us and doing what needs to be done that it's surprisingly easy to lose touch with what we actually feel or want.</p>
        <p>And even when we do have a bit of time to ourselves, how often do we spend it without some sort of distraction?</p>
        <p>I think we can become quite good at avoiding ourselves without even realising we're doing it.</p>
        <p>Which brings me to writing.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="stories-reading-image">
        <Image
          src={readingNook}
          alt="An open notebook and pen in a candlelit reading nook beside a fire"
          widths={[480, 720, 960, 1200, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 2 * var(--page-gutter)), 44vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={82}
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stories-section stories-section--writing" aria-labelledby="stories-writing-heading">
    <div class="stories-container stories-split stories-writing">
      <div class="stories-prose">
        <h2 id="stories-writing-heading">There's more than one way to write</h2>
        <p>I've journalled on and off for years. Sometimes it's been useful, although looking back, a fair amount of it involved writing down whatever was upsetting or frustrating me at the time.</p>
        <p>There's a place for that. Sometimes you just need to get the stuff out of your head and onto a page, without worrying whether it makes any sense.</p>
        <p>I've also written letters I never intended to send. I wrote one to my first dog when I was struggling with the grief of losing him. I wrote another to my mum after she died, although that came from a very different place. There were things I was angry about, and writing gave me somewhere to put them.</p>
        <p>Writing can serve all sorts of purposes, and it doesn't have to be beautifully constructed or even particularly coherent. Nobody needs to read it.</p>
        <p>But I've found another way of writing useful, too.</p>
        <p class="stories-emphasis">Starting with a question.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stories-writing__prompt">
        <JournalPromptGenerator compact={true} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stories-section stories-section--after-prompt">
    <div class="stories-container stories-prose stories-after-prompt">
      <h2 id="stories-invitation-heading">A little time with yourself</h2>
      <p>You don't need to be a writer, and you certainly don't need to be planning a memoir.</p>
      <p>All you need is a bit of curiosity, something to write with and a willingness to see what comes out.</p>
      <p>A question can take you somewhere you wouldn't necessarily have gone if you'd simply sat down to write about your day. It might challenge an assumption, bring back a memory or get you thinking about something you'd never considered particularly important.</p>
      <p>You might end up writing a load of waffle, but that's OK. It was in your head anyway! And every now and then, you might write something that makes you stop and think, ‘Hang on, where did that come from?’</p>
      <p>That's what gave me the idea for the writing prompt generator.</p>
      <p>I've put together a collection of prompts, some playful, some thoughtful and some that might take you into slightly more uncomfortable territory.</p>
      <p>You can spend five minutes with one or get completely carried away. If a prompt doesn't interest you, pick another. And if something feels too much, leave it. You get to decide what you explore and how far you go.</p>
      <p>Who knows? You might discover something about yourself you hadn't noticed before.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stories-section stories-section--sharing" aria-labelledby="stories-sharing-heading">
    <div class="stories-container stories-split stories-sharing">
      <h2 id="stories-sharing-heading">And what about sharing our stories?</h2>
      <div class="stories-prose">
        <p>Writing something down and sharing it with another person are two very different things.</p>
        <p>For years, I carried a lot of shame about my past. Even when I began writing my memoir, I was still leaving things out, sometimes without consciously realising it. Eventually, I came to understand how much energy I'd spent trying to keep certain parts of myself hidden.</p>
        <p>There's something powerful about being able to tell your story and be met with understanding rather than judgement. But that doesn't mean everyone deserves access to it.</p>
        <p>It's a subject I want to explore more, particularly how shame influences what we choose to reveal and what we keep to ourselves.</p>
        <a class="stories-journal-link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}journal/`}>Explore the Journal <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
